The choice between best-fit and first-fit allocation strategies depends on the distribution of memory requests. Best-fit is generally more suitable when requests are small or when there's a high probability that the optimal block size is smaller than the average block size in the pool, while first-fit performs better when the majority of requests are larger than the average block size in the pool. In this case, it is best to use first-fit as it involves fewer block splits and merges, leading to better performance under high load conditions.']

