[' Here is a C++ solution using the Double Ended Queue (deque) data structure from the Standard Template Library (STL) for efficient insertion, deletion, and iteration over records. The custom `LOG_EVENT_RECORD` struct and function prototypes are defined at the beginning, followed by the implementation of the dynamic list management function `LogEventList`.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <deque>
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdint>
#include <cstring>
#include <exception>

struct LOG_EVENT_RECORD {
    enum class EventType { Info, Error, Warning };
    std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point timestamp;
    EventType eventType;
    char data[1024]; // Adjust the size as needed for your specific use case
};

class LogEventList {
private:
    std::deque<LOG_EVENT_RECORD> logs;

public:
    void addRecord(const LOG_EVENT_RECORD& record) {
        logs.push_back(record);
    }

    void removeRecordsByTypeAndTimestamp(EventType eventType, const std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point& timestamp) {
        auto it = logs.begin();
        while (it != logs.end()) {
            if ((it->eventType == eventType) && (it->timestamp <= timestamp)) {
                it = logs.erase(it);
            } else {
                ++it;
            }
        }
    }

    void filterAndIterate(EventType eventType, const std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point& startTimestamp,
                           const std::chrono::steady_clock::time_point& endTimestamp) {
        auto it = logs.cbegin();
        while (it != logs.cend()) {
            if ((it->eventType == eventType) && (it->timestamp >= startTimestamp) && (it->timestamp <= endTimestamp)) {
                std::cout << "Event type: " << static_cast<int>(it->eventType) << ", Timestamp: "
                          << it->timestamp.time_since_epoch().count() * 1e-9 << ", Data: " << it->data << std::endl;
                ++it;
            } else {
                ++it;
            }
        }
    }
};
```

In the example above, I chose to use `std::deque` for its efficient insertion and deletion at both ends. The function `addRecord` adds a new record to the end of the deque, while `removeRecordsByTypeAndTimestamp` removes records that meet specific event type and timestamp conditions.

The function `filterAndIterate` filters the logs based on given event type, start timestamp, and end timestamp, then iterates over the matching records and prints them to the console. To ensure minimal memory fragmentation, the deque grows and shrinks as needed without requiring explicit resizing or reallocations.

This solution is meant to serve as a starting point for further optimizations, customization, and extension as per the requirements mentioned above.']

